{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:712172ac4da7643d22210be04c7bfa5d3353c74981bcbe8a77aa1d6a2a6b1f54","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.28},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONJUNCT A (ICE BofA MOVE avg >=105, 2027-2036) = 0.50. The controlling fact is the AUDITOR's and the quantification is FERMI's; the Reference-Class SCOUT is down-weighted hard. MOVE is the curve-weighted average of NORMALIZED implied vol on 1-month Treasury options (20/20/40/20 across 2y/5y/10y/30y) expressed in BASIS POINTS of annualized yield vol, so it is mechanically LEVEL-DEPENDENT: MOVE/nominal-10y ran ~33 in the 2010s (72 at 2.2%), ~31 in 2023 (130 at 4.2%) and ~18 in 2025-26 (75 at 4.2%) — the recent trough is a RELATIVE-vol compression, not a level effect. The Scout's 0.25 rests on \"the window starts from a volatility trough near 60\" while he simultaneously uses ~103 as the long-run mean and forecasts ~4.4% nominals; the ~103 was achieved at LOWER nominal levels, so the normalization runs in his answer's favour and he dropped it. His own inputs support 0.40-0.48. At the nominals my CQ4 implies (real 2.15 + breakeven ~2.4 ≈ 4.5-4.6% US, G7-weighted lower) the 105 bar needs a ratio of ~23-24: above today's 18, well below the 30-33 norm. Against: the ten-year-AVERAGE arithmetic is unforgiving — one 2023-style year at 150 plus nine at 90 averages 96; two at 150 plus eight at 90 averages 102 — so clearing 105 needs a durably higher ordinary-year baseline near 95-100 PLUS spikes, a regime shift rather than an episode. Index-continuity caveat preserved: the MOVE has had ownership and tenor-weighting/CMT-normalization revisions, so a 2027-2036 average is a splice. COHERENCE WITH MY OWN CQ4, which Fermi